Give the kernel this board's counter rate instead of the Raspberry Pi's.

pe_identify_machine.c picks the clock rates by SoC name, and this board
answers to "bcm2837-io" because that is the machine configuration the kernel
is built with. The branch is the Raspberry Pi 3's:

    } else if (!strcmp(gPESoCDeviceType, "bcm2837-io")) {
            mclk = 1200000000;
            hclk = mclk / 4;
            pclk = hclk / 2;
            tclk = 1000000;
    } else {
            use_dt = 1;
    }

Matching a name means use_dt stays clear, so the device tree's
timebase-frequency is never read: there is no way to correct this from the
ADT, only from here.

The Exynos 7570's architected counter does not run at 1 MHz. Samsung's own
device tree, the one description of this board that is known to be correct
because Android boots from it, says what it runs at:

    /timer
        compatible         "arm,armv8-timer"
        clock-frequency    26000000

Four board revisions ship in the stock boot image and all four agree. So the
kernel believes twenty-six seconds have passed for every real one. Nothing
announces itself; everything that measures a duration is simply wrong by that
factor, and the damage surfaces as unrelated-looking faults -- a wake rate
monitor counting wakes per unit time against a clock running twenty-six times
fast, and above it every timeout in userland short by the same factor, which
is a poor foundation for a startup that is a sequence of handshakes.

Three sources, in order. The boot argument tbfreq= wins, so a wrong value
here can be corrected from the command line without another build.
CNTFRQ_EL0 comes next: it is the architected answer to this question, and
where firmware has programmed it, it is better than anything written down.
The device tree's 26 MHz is the floor, which is the part that matters,
because an earlier version of this patch trusted CNTFRQ_EL0 alone and changed
nothing at all -- Samsung's EL3 firmware appears to leave the register at
zero for the non-secure world, which is exactly why Linux carries the
frequency in the device tree in the first place.

Each candidate is taken only if it is a believable counter rate, at least
1 MHz and at most 1 GHz, so a typo in tbfreq= cannot make things worse than
they were.

Nothing here can be seen. kprintf in pexpert has nowhere to go on a board
without a UART, which is how the first attempt at this came to be judged from
the clock in a syslog timestamp. The companion report lives in the
IOMobileFramebuffer shim's start(), which does reach the console: it prints
the rate that was chosen, CNTFRQ_EL0 as it actually reads, and the kernel's
own idea of how long it has been running, which can be held against a
wristwatch.

It was already known. The prose of iokit-secure-root-no-platform-kext.patch
records it as a separate problem, noted there because it explained why a
thirty second wait looked instant. This is that separate problem.
diff -ruN a/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c	2026-11-07 20:22:50.534595459 +0300
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_identify_machine.c	2026-11-07 20:22:50.536209715 +0300
@@ -101,6 +101,45 @@
 		hclk = mclk / 4;
 		pclk = hclk / 2;
 		tclk = 1000000;
+#if defined(__arm64__)
+		/*
+		 * The 1 MHz above is the Raspberry Pi 3's. This board only
+		 * borrows the BCM2837 machine configuration; its architected
+		 * counter runs at its own rate, and because the SoC name
+		 * matches here the device tree's timebase-frequency is never
+		 * consulted -- use_dt stays clear.
+		 *
+		 * Getting it wrong is not a small error. Every duration the
+		 * kernel computes is scaled by the ratio, so on a counter that
+		 * ticks at 26 MHz a deadline arrives twenty-six times too
+		 * early and every timeout in userland is short by the same
+		 * factor.
+		 *
+		 * 26 MHz is what Samsung's device tree says this SoC's timer
+		 * runs at, and it is the floor because CNTFRQ_EL0 reads zero
+		 * here: the EL3 firmware does not program it for the
+		 * non-secure world. Where firmware does program it, it is the
+		 * architected answer and wins over the written-down one; a
+		 * tbfreq= boot argument wins over both, so a wrong value can
+		 * be corrected without another build. Each candidate is taken
+		 * only if it is a believable counter rate.
+		 */
+		{
+			uint32_t tbarg = 0;
+			uint64_t cntfrq = 0;
+
+			tclk = 26000000;
+
+			__asm__ volatile ("mrs %0, CNTFRQ_EL0" : "=r" (cntfrq));
+			if (cntfrq >= 1000000ULL && cntfrq <= 1000000000ULL) {
+				tclk = (uint32_t)cntfrq;
+			}
+			if (PE_parse_boot_argn("tbfreq", &tbarg, sizeof(tbarg)) &&
+			    tbarg >= 1000000 && tbarg <= 1000000000) {
+				tclk = tbarg;
+			}
+		}
+#endif /* __arm64__ */
 	} else {
 		use_dt = 1;
 	}
